# New Code Standards: Enums and Helpers

Apply these rules to all newly written or substantially modified code.

## 1) Strings must be centralized in enums/constants

- Do not introduce repeated or business-meaningful string literals inline.
- Move such strings to a typed enum (preferred) or a shared constants object.
- Use the enum/constant reference at call sites.
- Exceptions: framework-required literals, third-party API contract keys, and one-off test fixtures.

Example:

```ts
// Avoid
if (status === "active") { ... }

// Prefer
enum EmploymentStatus {
  Active = "active",
}
if (status === EmploymentStatus.Active) { ... }
```

## 2) Reusable functions must live in helpers

- If a function is reused or likely to be reused, extract it from component/handler files.
- Place it in a co-located `*Helpers.ts`/`*Utils.ts` module (or existing shared helper module).
- Keep helper functions pure when possible and strongly typed.
- Components/pages should orchestrate state/UI; shared logic belongs in helpers.
- Default behavior: if code can reasonably be split into a reusable helper, split it now instead of waiting for duplication.
- Do not keep complex transformation/formatting/conditional logic inline in JSX or page components when it can be extracted.
